import json, re
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
stories=[]
def add(title,author,year,date,era,genre,setting,hook,cast,scenes,question,answer,source=1,note=''):
    chars=[dict(name=n,role=r) for n,r in (c.split('=',1) for c in cast.split('|'))]
    frames=[]
    for line in scenes.strip().split('\n'):
        heading,text,people,motif,cue=line.split('~')
        frames.append(dict(title=heading,text=text,cast=people.split(','),motif=motif,cue=cue))
    stories.append(dict(id=re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9]+','-',title.lower()).strip('-'),title=title,author=author,year=year,date=date,era=era,genre=genre,setting=setting,hook=hook,characters=chars,scenes=frames,question=question,answer=answer,source=source,note=note))
    add.last=stories[-1]

add('The Odyssey','Homer',-700,'c. 8th century BCE','ancient','Epic','Mediterranean and Ithaca','A long voyage is really a struggle to find home.','Odysseus=Clever traveller and king of Ithaca|Penelope=His resourceful wife|Telemachus=Their son|Athena=Divine helper|Polyphemus=The Cyclops', '''
A home without its king~Odysseus is still absent after Troy. Suitors consume his household while Penelope delays remarriage and Telemachus searches for news.~Penelope,Telemachus~home~HOME waits
Nobody escapes~Odysseus blinds the Cyclops and escapes beneath sheep. His boast reveals his name, bringing Poseidon's anger upon him.~Odysseus,Polyphemus~cave~CLEVERNESS has a cost
Temptations at sea~Circe, the Sirens and the dangerous straits test the travellers. His companions kill the Sun god's cattle and perish; Odysseus survives alone.~Odysseus~sea~TEMPTATION destroys the crew
A hidden return~After Calypso releases him and the Phaeacians help him, Odysseus reaches Ithaca. Athena disguises him as a beggar; he reunites with his son.~Odysseus,Telemachus,Athena~home~DISGUISE before recognition
The bow and the bed~Odysseus wins the bow contest and kills the suitors. Penelope's test of their immovable marriage bed confirms his identity; peace eventually follows.~Odysseus,Penelope~bow~HOME is recognised''','What proves Odysseus is truly Penelope\'s husband?','His knowledge of their marriage bed, built around a living olive tree.',source='https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1728/1728-h/1728-h.htm',note='Traditional approximate date; oral composition and authorship are debated. Scenes follow the remembered journey rather than the epic\'s nonlinear telling.')
add('Antigone','Sophocles',-441,'c. 441 BCE','ancient','Tragedy','Thebes','One burial pits conscience against the state.','Antigone=Defies the burial ban|Creon=King enforcing his decree|Ismene=Antigone\'s cautious sister|Haemon=Creon\'s son and Antigone\'s fiancé|Tiresias=Prophet warning the king', '''
Two brothers dead~After civil war, Creon honours Eteocles but forbids burial for Polynices. Antigone believes the dead deserve sacred duties beyond a king's decree.~Antigone,Ismene,Creon~crown~LAW versus DUTY
The forbidden burial~Ismene fears resistance. Antigone nevertheless covers her brother's body and is caught; she openly owns her action.~Antigone,Creon~grave~CONSCIENCE acts
The king refuses~Haemon asks his father to listen. Creon treats disagreement as disloyalty and orders Antigone sealed in a rocky tomb.~Creon,Haemon,Antigone~cave~PRIDE will not listen
The warning arrives~Tiresias warns that the gods reject Thebes' offerings. Creon finally yields and goes to bury Polynices and free Antigone.~Tiresias,Creon~storm~CHANGE comes late
Too late to save them~Antigone has hanged herself. Haemon dies by suicide, followed by his mother Eurydice. Creon survives to bear the consequences of his rigidity.~Creon,Haemon~grave~DELAY becomes loss''','Why does Antigone oppose Creon?','She places sacred obligations to the dead above the king\'s burial prohibition.',source='https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14484/pg14484-images.html',note='First performance traditionally dated around 441 BCE; not a modern publication date.')
add('Beowulf','Anonymous',1000,'c. 8th–11th century; manuscript c. 1000','medieval','Heroic epic','Denmark and Geatland','Three monsters measure a hero\'s whole life.','Beowulf=Geatish warrior then king|Hrothgar=Danish king|Grendel=Attacker of Heorot|Grendel\'s mother=Seeks revenge|Wiglaf=Loyal young warrior', '''
The hall falls silent~Grendel attacks Hrothgar's feast hall, Heorot. Beowulf sails from Geatland to help the Danish king.~Beowulf,Hrothgar,Grendel~hall~HALL needs a hero
Strength without a sword~Beowulf meets Grendel barehanded and tears away his arm. The monster flees to die, and the hall celebrates.~Beowulf,Grendel~sword~FIRST monster defeated
Beneath the mere~Grendel's mother avenges her son. Beowulf descends into her underwater hall and kills her with a giant sword.~Beowulf,Grendel's mother~cave~REVENGE beneath the water
The last battle~Decades later, King Beowulf confronts a dragon angered by a stolen cup. Only Wiglaf stays to help; the dragon dies, but Beowulf is mortally wounded.~Beowulf,Wiglaf~dragon~LOYALTY at the end
The funeral mound~The Geats bury their king with treasure. The poem ends in mourning and fear for a people who have lost their protector.~Wiglaf~grave~GLORY cannot prevent death''','Which warrior stays with Beowulf against the dragon?','Wiglaf. His loyalty contrasts with the warriors who flee.',note='Composition is uncertain. The manuscript date provides the timeline anchor; the story is set much earlier.')
add('The Canterbury Tales','Geoffrey Chaucer',1387,'c. 1387–1400','medieval','Frame narrative and satire','A pilgrimage from London to Canterbury','A pilgrimage becomes a portrait of society.','Harry Bailly=Host and storyteller judge|The Knight=Chivalric pilgrim|The Miller=Earthy comic storyteller|The Wife of Bath=Experienced challenger of authority|The Pardoner=Seller of pardons', '''
At the Tabard~Pilgrims gather at an inn. Harry Bailly proposes a tale-telling contest during their journey to Canterbury.~Harry Bailly,The Knight,The Wife of Bath~hall~PILGRIMAGE becomes stories
Order meets disruption~The Knight tells of noble rivals in love. The drunken Miller answers with a comic tale of desire, trickery and a deceived carpenter.~The Knight,The Miller~road~HIGH meets LOW
Who holds authority?~The Wife of Bath uses her marriages to challenge male authority. Her tale asks what women most desire and answers with sovereignty.~The Wife of Bath~crown~SOVEREIGNTY
A sermon against greed~The Pardoner admits his own corruption yet tells of three men who seek Death, find gold, betray each other and die.~The Pardoner~coins~GREED destroys
An unfinished journey~The surviving collection does not complete the proposed contest. The voices expose tensions among social rank, religion, gender and desire.~Harry Bailly,The Wife of Bath,The Pardoner~book~THE TELLER shapes the tale''','Why is this a frame narrative?','A pilgrimage and storytelling contest form the outer story that contains the individual tales.')
add('The Spanish Tragedy','Thomas Kyd',1587,'c. 1587–1592','renaissance','Revenge tragedy','Spain and Portugal','A grieving father turns theatre into revenge.','Hieronimo=Father seeking justice|Horatio=His murdered son|Bel-imperia=Woman who loves Horatio|Lorenzo=Plotter behind the murder|Balthazar=Portuguese prince and rival', '''
A ghost watches~Don Andrea's ghost and Revenge watch the living. Balthazar, captured in war, becomes entangled in Spanish court politics.~Balthazar,Lorenzo~ghost~REVENGE watches
Love in the garden~Bel-imperia loves Horatio. Lorenzo and Balthazar arrange his murder, and Hieronimo finds his son's body hanging in the garden.~Horatio,Bel-imperia,Hieronimo~grave~LOVE becomes grief
Justice is blocked~Bel-imperia sends evidence. Hieronimo struggles with grief and the court's obstruction; Isabella, his wife, is also destroyed by sorrow.~Hieronimo,Bel-imperia,Lorenzo~letter~JUSTICE denied
A play within the play~Hieronimo stages a performance with the guilty nobles. Real weapons turn the acted killings into actual revenge.~Hieronimo,Bel-imperia,Balthazar~stage~THEATRE becomes a weapon
The final silence~Lorenzo and Balthazar die. Bel-imperia and Hieronimo also die; the ghostly frame gives the bloodshed an afterlife conclusion.~Hieronimo,Bel-imperia~grave~REVENGE consumes the revenger''','How does Hieronimo carry out his revenge?','He uses a play within the play, replacing theatrical violence with real killings.',source=3,note='Approximate composition or early performance range; dating is uncertain.')
add('The Faerie Queene','Edmund Spenser',1590,'1590 and 1596','renaissance','Allegorical epic','An imagined realm of knights and trials','Knights make moral virtues visible.','Redcrosse=Knight of holiness|Una=Truth|Duessa=Falsehood|Guyon=Temperance|Britomart=Chastity|Arthur=Magnificence and helper', '''
Truth and its knight~Redcrosse travels with Una to free her parents from a dragon. His adventure tests holiness rather than physical strength alone.~Redcrosse,Una~road~HOLINESS needs truth
False appearances~Archimago's deception separates Redcrosse from Una. Duessa offers a misleading substitute for truth, and the knight falls into error.~Redcrosse,Duessa~mirror~FALSEHOOD deceives
Fall and restoration~Orgoglio imprisons Redcrosse; Arthur helps rescue him. The House of Holiness prepares him to confront the dragon.~Redcrosse,Arthur,Una~castle~HELP restores virtue
The dragon defeated~Redcrosse defeats the dragon across three days. His promised union with Una represents holiness joined to truth.~Redcrosse,Una~dragon~TRUTH and HOLINESS
The other virtues~Guyon tests temperance and Britomart chastity. Later books explore friendship, justice and courtesy; the planned twelve-book project remains incomplete.~Guyon,Britomart,Arthur~book~SIX completed books''','What does Una represent?','Truth. Redcrosse\'s journey requires learning to remain faithful to her.')
add('Doctor Faustus','Christopher Marlowe',1592,'c. 1592; printed 1604','renaissance','Tragedy','Wittenberg and a supernatural world','Twenty-four years of power cannot buy back a soul.','Faustus=Scholar hungry for power|Mephistopheles=Devil serving Lucifer|Lucifer=Claims Faustus\'s soul|Good Angel=Urges repentance|Old Man=Offers a final moral warning', '''
Knowledge is not enough~Faustus dismisses the limits of established learning and turns to magic, imagining extraordinary power and knowledge.~Faustus~book~AMBITION beyond limits
The bargain~He signs a pact in blood: twenty-four years of Mephistopheles' service in exchange for his soul. Warnings do not stop him.~Faustus,Mephistopheles,Lucifer~contract~POWER on a deadline
Power shrinks into spectacle~Instead of transforming the world, Faustus often uses magic for tricks and entertainment. His grand ambitions become small performances.~Faustus,Mephistopheles~stage~GREAT dreams small tricks
Repentance postponed~Good counsel and the Old Man urge repentance. Faustus repeatedly hesitates, while spectacle and demonic intimidation draw him back.~Faustus,Good Angel,Old Man~clock~DELAY closes the door
The final hour~Facing the end of his bargain, Faustus longs to stop time. Devils take him away, and the scholars discover his remains.~Faustus,Mephistopheles~clock~TIME cannot be purchased''','What is the central bargain?','Twenty-four years of supernatural service in exchange for Faustus\'s soul.',source=3,note='Composition is disputed; the supplied notes use c. 1592. A-text published 1604 and B-text 1616.')
add('Othello','William Shakespeare',1604,'c. 1603–1604','renaissance','Tragedy','Venice and Cyprus','A planted token turns trust into jealousy.','Othello=General and Desdemona\'s husband|Desdemona=Faithful wife|Iago=Manipulative ensign|Cassio=Lieutenant|Emilia=Iago\'s wife and Desdemona\'s attendant', '''
A marriage defended~Othello and Desdemona defend their secret marriage in Venice. Othello is sent to Cyprus as a military threat approaches.~Othello,Desdemona~ship~LOVE under pressure
Iago opens the trap~Resentful of Cassio's promotion, Iago engineers a drunken fight. Cassio loses his position and seeks Desdemona's help.~Iago,Cassio~sword~MANIPULATION begins
The handkerchief~Emilia picks up Desdemona's dropped handkerchief. Iago plants it with Cassio and encourages Othello to treat it as proof of adultery.~Iago,Emilia,Othello~cloth~TOKEN becomes false proof
Jealousy becomes murder~Othello trusts Iago over Desdemona's protestations. He smothers his innocent wife, imagining that he is delivering justice.~Othello,Desdemona~bed~TRUST destroyed
The truth arrives~Emilia exposes the plot; Iago kills her. Othello recognises his error and kills himself, while Iago is taken into custody.~Othello,Emilia,Iago~grave~TRUTH comes too late''','Why is the handkerchief so important?','Iago manipulates its movement to make an innocent object appear to prove Desdemona\'s infidelity.',source=2,note='Othello is described as a Moor; the play does not establish a precise modern nationality or religious biography.')
add('Macbeth','William Shakespeare',1606,'c. 1606','renaissance','Tragedy','Scotland','A crown won by murder cannot bring peace.','Macbeth=Warrior who becomes a tyrant|Lady Macbeth=His ambitious wife|Banquo=Warrior and prophecy recipient|Duncan=King of Scotland|Macduff=Opponent who defeats Macbeth', '''
The prophecy~Three witches predict Macbeth's kingship and Banquo's royal descendants. Macbeth begins to imagine taking the crown.~Macbeth,Banquo~crown~PROPHECY tempts
Duncan is murdered~Lady Macbeth pushes Macbeth toward action. He kills the sleeping Duncan, and suspicion drives Duncan's sons away.~Macbeth,Lady Macbeth,Duncan~dagger~AMBITION acts
A ghost at the feast~Macbeth orders Banquo killed, but Fleance escapes. Banquo's ghost disrupts the royal feast, making guilt publicly visible.~Macbeth,Banquo~ghost~GUILT returns
False security~The witches' equivocal assurances encourage Macbeth. He has Macduff's family murdered; Lady Macbeth later sleepwalks, haunted by imagined blood.~Macbeth,Lady Macbeth~mirror~TYRANNY isolates
The promises unravel~Malcolm's army carries branches from Birnam Wood. Macduff, born by Caesarean delivery, kills Macbeth; Malcolm becomes king.~Macbeth,Macduff~forest~EQUIVOCATION exposed''','How does Birnam Wood move?','Soldiers camouflage themselves with branches, fulfilling the prophecy in an unexpected way.',source=2)
add('The Tempest','William Shakespeare',1611,'c. 1610–1611','renaissance','Romance and tragicomedy','A Mediterranean island','A magician wins by giving up control.','Prospero=Exiled duke and magician|Miranda=His daughter|Ariel=Spirit seeking freedom|Caliban=Inhabitant subjected to Prospero|Ferdinand=Prince who loves Miranda|Antonio=Prospero\'s usurping brother', '''
The manufactured storm~Prospero orders Ariel to raise a storm that brings his old enemies to the island. He tells Miranda how Antonio took his dukedom.~Prospero,Ariel,Miranda~storm~REVENGE stages a meeting
Love under supervision~Miranda and Ferdinand fall in love. Prospero tests Ferdinand through labour while directing much of what happens on the island.~Miranda,Ferdinand,Prospero~heart~LOVE amid control
Two plots~Antonio and Sebastian plot against Alonso. Elsewhere Caliban joins Stephano and Trinculo in a plan against Prospero; Ariel frustrates both conspiracies.~Caliban,Ariel,Antonio~dagger~POWER breeds plots
The choice to forgive~Ariel's compassion helps move Prospero. Rather than complete his revenge, he confronts his enemies and chooses forgiveness.~Prospero,Ariel,Antonio~mirror~COMPASSION changes power
Magic surrendered~Prospero restores relations, frees Ariel and promises to abandon magic. The ending leaves difficult questions about Caliban and colonial power open.~Prospero,Ariel,Caliban~book~FREEDOM through renunciation''','What does Prospero give up at the end?','His magic and control over Ariel, as part of moving from revenge toward reconciliation.',source=2)
add('Paradise Lost','John Milton',1667,'1667; revised 1674','restoration','Biblical epic','Hell, Heaven and Eden','Disobedience loses a garden but does not end hope.','Satan=Rebel seeking revenge|Adam=First man|Eve=First woman|Raphael=Warns the humans|Michael=Reveals the future', '''
Defeat in Heaven~After rebellion, Satan and his followers lie in Hell. They gather at Pandemonium and plan to attack God's new human creation.~Satan~fire~REBELLION becomes revenge
The garden and the warning~Adam and Eve live in Eden with freedom and a prohibition. Raphael recounts the rebellion and warns them against deception.~Adam,Eve,Raphael~garden~FREEDOM includes choice
The serpent persuades~Satan approaches Eve through the serpent. He promises knowledge and elevation, and she eats the forbidden fruit.~Eve,Satan~apple~TEMPTATION disguises itself
Adam chooses to follow~Adam knowingly eats after Eve. Shame, accusation and estrangement replace their earlier harmony; the Fall has consequences beyond the pair.~Adam,Eve~apple~LOVE cannot excuse disobedience
Leaving with hope~After repentance, Michael shows Adam future suffering and redemption. Adam and Eve leave Eden together, facing a world in which providence still guides them.~Adam,Eve,Michael~gate~LOSS with a future''','Why does Adam eat the fruit?','He knowingly follows Eve, unable to bear separation from her; his choice remains disobedience.',note='Combines the accounts in Literature Part 1 and Literature part 3. Ten books in 1667; twelve in 1674.')
add('To His Coy Mistress','Andrew Marvell',1681,'1681; probably composed in the 1650s','restoration','Metaphysical lyric','An imagined conversation between lovers','If there were time—but there is not—therefore act.','The speaker=Argues for immediate intimacy|The mistress=The silent addressee', '''
If time were unlimited~The speaker imagines that endless space and time would permit leisurely admiration and an almost limitless courtship.~The speaker,The mistress~garden~IF infinite time
But time pursues us~He interrupts the fantasy with mortality. Life is short, and the grave offers neither intimacy nor the pleasures he imagines.~The speaker~clock~BUT mortality
Therefore seize the present~He urges the mistress to join him in passionate action. The argument transforms fear of time into pressure to act now.~The speaker,The mistress~heart~THEREFORE act
Remember the argument~This is a persuasive lyric, not a narrated romance with a recorded response. Its wit and urgency can also be read as manipulative.~The speaker,The mistress~book~QUESTION the persuasion''','What are the poem\'s three argumentative movements?','If we had unlimited time; but we face death; therefore seize the present.',source=2,note='Placed by first publication, not likely composition. The mistress never supplies a spoken answer in the poem.')
add('The Way of the World','William Congreve',1700,'1700','restoration','Comedy of manners','Fashionable London','Love must negotiate inheritance and independence.','Mirabell=Plans to marry Millamant|Millamant=Wants love without losing autonomy|Lady Wishfort=Controls inheritance|Fainall=Scheming blackmailer|Mrs. Fainall=His wife|Waitwell=Mirabell\'s servant', '''
Love needs consent~Mirabell and Millamant want to marry, but her inheritance depends on Lady Wishfort's approval. Society makes love a financial negotiation.~Mirabell,Millamant,Lady Wishfort~coins~LOVE meets inheritance
The false suitor~Mirabell has Waitwell marry Foible before posing as Sir Rowland. The plan is to entrap Lady Wishfort and then bargain for her approval.~Mirabell,Waitwell,Lady Wishfort~mask~DECEPTION as strategy
The proviso scene~Millamant and Mirabell negotiate conditions for marriage. Wit protects personal space and challenges automatic marital domination.~Millamant,Mirabell~contract~INDEPENDENCE within marriage
Fainall's threat~Fainall threatens scandal over his wife's earlier relationship with Mirabell and attempts to gain control of the women's money.~Fainall,Mrs. Fainall,Lady Wishfort~letter~REPUTATION weaponised
The document defeats him~A trust deed already protects Mrs. Fainall's property through Mirabell. Fainall's plan fails, clearing the way for the lovers' marriage.~Mirabell,Millamant,Mrs. Fainall~contract~PROPERTY protected''','What protects Mrs. Fainall\'s money?','An earlier trust deed placed her property beyond her husband\'s attempted seizure.',source=2)
add('The Rape of the Lock','Alexander Pope',1712,'1712; expanded 1714','restoration','Mock epic','Aristocratic social life around London','A stolen curl becomes a ridiculously grand war.','Belinda=Fashionable heroine|The Baron=Cuts her lock of hair|Ariel=Guardian sylph|Umbriel=Gnome who fuels distress|Clarissa=Provides scissors and counsel', '''
An epic dressing table~Belinda prepares for society as if arming for battle. Ariel and the sylphs surround her with supernatural protection.~Belinda,Ariel~mirror~BEAUTY becomes armour
Cards as warfare~At Hampton Court, a game of ombre receives the language of heroic battle. Belinda's social triumph has epic proportions.~Belinda,The Baron~cards~SMALL stakes epic style
The scissors strike~With Clarissa's scissors, the Baron cuts a lock from Belinda's hair. The poem treats the taking as a mighty catastrophe.~Belinda,The Baron,Clarissa~scissors~A CURL becomes a crisis
Distress becomes combat~Umbriel brings exaggerated sorrow and anger. A fashionable quarrel escalates into mock warfare, despite Clarissa's advice about good sense.~Belinda,Umbriel,Clarissa~storm~VANITY magnifies loss
A lock among the stars~The missing lock receives an imagined celestial afterlife. Pope immortalises the trivial event while satirising social pride.~Belinda,The Baron~stars~TRIVIAL becomes immortal''','Why is this a mock epic?','It applies the grand language and machinery of epic poetry to the comparatively small event of a stolen lock.',note='The historical title uses rape in the sense of taking or seizure. The plot concerns an unauthorised cutting of hair.')
add('Lyrical Ballads','William Wordsworth and S. T. Coleridge',1798,'1798; Preface 1800','romantic','Poetry collection','Ordinary lives, remembered landscapes and supernatural voyages','Everyday feeling and strange wonder reshape poetry.','Wordsworth=Poet of ordinary life and memory|Coleridge=Poet of supernatural imagination|The Ancient Mariner=Voyager burdened by guilt|The speaker at Tintern Abbey=Reflects on nature and memory', '''
A new poetic direction~The collection brings humble lives and strong feeling into poetry. Wordsworth's later Preface explains the use of language associated with ordinary people.~Wordsworth,Coleridge~book~ORDINARY becomes poetic
The albatross is killed~In Coleridge's Ancient Mariner, a sailor kills a helpful bird. A stranded ship and supernatural punishment follow the apparently senseless act.~The Ancient Mariner~ship~ACTION leads to guilt
Blessing living things~The Mariner spontaneously blesses water snakes. The albatross falls from his neck, beginning a difficult movement toward release.~The Ancient Mariner~sea~LOVE opens renewal
Return to Tintern Abbey~Wordsworth's speaker revisits a landscape after years away. Remembered nature has sustained him and now connects him to his sister Dorothy.~The speaker at Tintern Abbey~forest~MEMORY sustains
The poems work differently~This collection has no single continuous plot. Remember the partnership: ordinary experience made fresh, and supernatural experience made emotionally credible.~Wordsworth,Coleridge~book~TWO approaches one turning point''','How do the two poets\' approaches differ?','Wordsworth often renews everyday experience; Coleridge gives supernatural events emotional credibility.',note='Collection overview and representative poems, not a single invented storyline.')
add('The Excursion','William Wordsworth',1814,'1814','romantic','Philosophical narrative poem','A rural landscape in England','Walking and conversation seek an answer to despair.','The Poet=Narrator and listener|The Wanderer=Experienced advocate of hope|The Solitary=Disillusioned recluse|The Pastor=Guide through local lives', '''
The journey begins~The Poet meets the Wanderer. Their movement through the landscape opens a sustained discussion about suffering and the meaning of human life.~The Poet,The Wanderer~road~WALK and listen
Margaret's story~The Wanderer recalls Margaret, whose domestic hopes collapse through hardship and loss. The ruined cottage turns absence into a visible memory.~The Wanderer,The Poet~home~RUIN holds a life
The Solitary's despair~They meet a man wounded by bereavement and disappointed political hopes. He doubts the consolations offered by faith and society.~The Solitary,The Wanderer~mountain~DESPAIR questions hope
Lives in the churchyard~The Pastor recounts local people and their deaths. Individual histories broaden the argument beyond one person's sorrow.~The Pastor,The Poet,The Solitary~grave~COMMUNITY gives perspective
Hope remains a discussion~Nature, fellowship and religious belief offer responses to isolation. The poem develops an ongoing philosophical dialogue rather than a tidy adventure ending.~The Wanderer,The Solitary,The Pastor~forest~MEANING through fellowship''','What is the main narrative method?','Walking, conversation and remembered lives, through which the speakers debate suffering and hope.',source=2)
add('Frankenstein','Mary Shelley',1818,'1818; revised 1831','romantic','Gothic novel','Geneva, the Alps and the Arctic','Creating life is only the beginning of responsibility.','Victor Frankenstein=Creator who abandons his creature|The creature=Intelligent being seeking belonging|Elizabeth=Victor\'s beloved|Robert Walton=Arctic explorer and frame narrator|Henry Clerval=Victor\'s friend', '''
An Arctic warning~Walton rescues Victor in the Arctic. Victor tells how scientific ambition led him to create life and then recoil from what he had made.~Robert Walton,Victor Frankenstein~ship~AMBITION tells its cost
Life abandoned~The creature awakens, but Victor flees. Without care or acceptance, the being learns language and observes a family from hiding.~Victor Frankenstein,The creature~laboratory~CREATION needs care
Rejection turns violent~Rejected despite his longing for companionship, the creature kills Victor's brother William and frames Justine. He later demands a companion of his own.~The creature,Victor Frankenstein~forest~EXCLUSION breeds revenge
The promise is broken~Victor begins a second creature but destroys it. The first retaliates, murdering Clerval and later Elizabeth on Victor's wedding night.~Victor Frankenstein,The creature,Elizabeth~bed~BROKEN promise deepens loss
Pursuit into ice~Victor pursues his creation north and dies aboard Walton's ship. The creature mourns, announces his own intended death and disappears; Walton turns back.~Victor Frankenstein,The creature,Robert Walton~ice~RESPONSIBILITY remembered''','Who is Frankenstein?','Victor, the creator. His creature is not given a personal name.',source='https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/41445',note='Summary based on the 1818 text. The ending reports the creature\'s intention; his death is not witnessed.')
add('Vanity Fair','William Makepeace Thackeray',1847,'1847–1848','victorian','Satirical novel','Britain and Europe around the Napoleonic wars','Two women expose a marketplace of status.','Becky Sharp=Resourceful social climber|Amelia Sedley=Devoted but idealising friend|Rawdon Crawley=Becky\'s husband|George Osborne=Amelia\'s husband|William Dobbin=Loyal lover of Amelia', '''
Two friends leave school~Becky seeks advancement with few advantages; Amelia seeks happiness through love. Their contrasting ambitions enter a society ruled by money and status.~Becky Sharp,Amelia Sedley~road~AMBITION versus sentiment
Two marriages~Becky secretly marries Rawdon, disrupting inheritance hopes. Amelia marries George despite family opposition, while Dobbin quietly helps her.~Becky Sharp,Rawdon Crawley,Amelia Sedley~heart~MARRIAGE meets money
Waterloo divides their lives~George dies at Waterloo. Amelia grieves and idealises him, while Becky continues pursuing connections and social elevation.~George Osborne,Amelia Sedley,Becky Sharp~sword~WAR changes the path
Society's glitter cracks~Becky's association with Lord Steyne ends in scandal and the collapse of her marriage. Status proves fragile and morally compromised.~Becky Sharp,Rawdon Crawley~mirror~APPEARANCE collapses
Loyalty finally recognised~Amelia learns evidence of George's unfaithfulness and comes to value Dobbin. Becky survives, while the novel's marketplace of vanity continues.~Amelia Sedley,William Dobbin,Becky Sharp~letter~CHARACTER beyond glamour''','What does the title\'s fair suggest?','A marketplace in which people trade money, marriage, reputation and status.',source=3)
add('In Memoriam A.H.H.','Alfred, Lord Tennyson',1850,'1850','victorian','Elegy','Memory, changing seasons and landscapes of grief','Grief circles through doubt toward guarded hope.','The poetic speaker=Mourns and questions|Arthur Henry Hallam=Lost friend and subject of the elegy', '''
The absent friend~Hallam's death begins an extended work of mourning. Familiar places become painful reminders of someone who can no longer return.~The poetic speaker,Arthur Henry Hallam~grave~LOSS begins the circle
Seasons return~Christmases and anniversaries recur. Time changes the speaker's grief without simply erasing it.~The poetic speaker~clock~TIME revisits grief
Nature unsettles faith~The speaker confronts mortality and the apparent indifference of nature. Scientific and geological questions complicate belief in a benevolent order.~The poetic speaker~forest~DOUBT enters faith
Love survives uncertainty~Memory and affection support tentative trust even where proof is unavailable. The poem repeatedly revises earlier emotional positions.~The poetic speaker,Arthur Henry Hallam~heart~LOVE withstands doubt
A wedding opens the future~The epilogue celebrates a marriage, bringing continuity and hope alongside the enduring memory of Hallam.~The poetic speaker~garden~HOPE with remembrance''','Is the poem a straight sequence from sadness to happiness?','No. Grief, doubt and hope recur across its lyrics; the journey is not a simple linear cure.',note='Published before Darwin\'s On the Origin of Species in 1859. These frames are thematic landmarks rather than a continuous plot.')
add('The Return of the Native','Thomas Hardy',1878,'1878','victorian','Tragic novel','Egdon Heath in Wessex','He wants to return; she wants to escape.','Clym Yeobright=Returning idealist|Eustacia Vye=Dreams of escape|Damon Wildeve=Unreliable lover|Thomasin Yeobright=Clym\'s cousin|Mrs. Yeobright=Clym\'s mother|Diggory Venn=Loyal supporter of Thomasin', '''
Two opposite dreams~Clym returns from Paris hoping to educate local people. Eustacia sees him as a possible route away from Egdon Heath.~Clym Yeobright,Eustacia Vye~road~RETURN versus ESCAPE
An unstable triangle~Eustacia's earlier connection to Wildeve complicates his attachment to Thomasin. Diggory Venn quietly protects Thomasin's interests.~Eustacia Vye,Damon Wildeve,Thomasin Yeobright~heart~DESIRE crosses loyalty
Marriage without a shared future~Clym marries Eustacia against his mother's wishes. Overstudy damages his eyesight, and he turns to furze-cutting rather than fulfilling his educational plan.~Clym Yeobright,Eustacia Vye,Mrs. Yeobright~home~DREAMS diverge
The unopened door~Mrs. Yeobright visits while Clym sleeps and Wildeve is inside. Eustacia fails to admit her; exhausted and believing herself rejected, the mother dies after an adder bite.~Mrs. Yeobright,Eustacia Vye,Clym Yeobright~gate~MISUNDERSTANDING kills
Storm at the weir~The marriage breaks down. Eustacia and Wildeve drown on a stormy night; Thomasin eventually marries Venn, while Clym becomes a preacher.~Eustacia Vye,Damon Wildeve,Diggory Venn~storm~THE HEATH remains''','What makes Clym and Eustacia\'s marriage unstable?','He wants a meaningful life on the heath; she imagines him taking her away from it.',note='Clym plans to teach but eyesight problems prevent the project. Eustacia\'s death remains ambiguous rather than an established suicide.')
add('A Doll’s House','Henrik Ibsen',1879,'1879','victorian','Realist drama','The Helmer home in Norway','A letter turns a comfortable home into a cage.','Nora Helmer=Wife with a secret debt|Torvald Helmer=Husband concerned with respectability|Krogstad=Lender and bank employee|Kristine Linde=Nora\'s independent friend|Dr. Rank=Ill family friend', '''
Christmas and appearances~Nora seems cheerful in a comfortable marriage. Torvald's affectionate pet names conceal a relationship in which he holds authority.~Nora Helmer,Torvald Helmer~home~PERFECT appearance
The secret loan~Nora borrowed to finance a trip that saved Torvald's health, forging her father's signature. Krogstad threatens exposure when Torvald plans to dismiss him.~Nora Helmer,Krogstad~contract~SACRIFICE becomes leverage
Dancing against time~Nora uses the tarantella to delay Torvald from reading the revealing letter. Kristine and Krogstad renew their earlier relationship.~Nora Helmer,Kristine Linde,Krogstad~stage~PERFORMANCE hides panic
The husband's response~Torvald reads the letter and condemns Nora, concentrating on his reputation. When the evidence is returned, he wants ordinary life restored.~Nora Helmer,Torvald Helmer~letter~REPUTATION before care
The door closes~Nora sees that she has been treated as a doll by father and husband. She leaves husband and children to educate herself and discover her own identity.~Nora Helmer,Torvald Helmer~gate~SELFHOOD walks out''','What destroys Nora\'s belief in her marriage?','Torvald\'s response to the letter reveals that he prioritises reputation over understanding her sacrifice.',source=3,note='The play does not establish that Nora\'s departure is temporary.')
add('Tess of the d’Urbervilles','Thomas Hardy',1891,'1891','victorian','Tragic social novel','Rural Wessex','Social judgement punishes a woman already harmed.','Tess Durbeyfield=Young woman facing exploitation|Alec d\'Urberville=Exploits and pursues Tess|Angel Clare=Lover with a double standard|Sorrow=Tess\'s infant son', '''
A name promises fortune~The Durbeyfields learn of noble ancestry. Poverty leads them to send Tess to the wealthy d'Urberville household, where she meets Alec.~Tess Durbeyfield,Alec d'Urberville~home~CLASS creates vulnerability
Harm and loss~Alec sexually exploits Tess. She returns home and gives birth to Sorrow, who dies in infancy; community judgement deepens her suffering.~Tess Durbeyfield,Alec d'Urberville,Sorrow~grave~VICTIM is judged
A dairy and a new hope~At Talbothays, Tess and Angel fall in love. Tess struggles to disclose her past, hoping their marriage will offer a different future.~Tess Durbeyfield,Angel Clare~garden~LOVE offers hope
The double standard~On their wedding night Angel admits his own sexual past but rejects Tess after hearing hers. Poverty and abandonment leave her vulnerable to Alec again.~Tess Durbeyfield,Angel Clare,Alec d'Urberville~letter~FORGIVENESS unequal
Too late for rescue~Angel returns, but Tess is living with Alec. She kills Alec, briefly flees with Angel, is arrested at Stonehenge and is executed.~Tess Durbeyfield,Angel Clare,Alec d'Urberville~stone~INJUSTICE closes in''','What is Angel\'s double standard?','He expects forgiveness for his sexual past while refusing to accept Tess\'s history of exploitation.',note='The encounter with Alec is narrated elliptically; this adaptation avoids presenting Tess as responsible for her exploitation.')
add('Heart of Darkness','Joseph Conrad',1899,'1899 serial; 1902 book','modern','Novella','The Thames frame and colonial Congo','An upriver journey exposes the violence of empire.','Marlow=Narrator travelling upriver|Kurtz=Ivory agent with terrifying power|The Manager=Company authority|The Intended=Kurtz\'s fiancée in Europe', '''
A story on the Thames~Marlow recounts his employment by a European trading company. The voyage into Congo begins beneath ideals of civilisation and progress.~Marlow~ship~IDEALS frame empire
The machinery of cruelty~At company stations he sees exploitation, dying labourers and waste. The civilising language hides a brutal hunger for ivory.~Marlow,The Manager~chains~PROGRESS masks violence
Upriver toward Kurtz~Kurtz's reputation grows while Marlow travels through uncertainty and an attack. Kurtz's extraordinary influence becomes the object of his search.~Marlow,Kurtz~river~REPUTATION becomes obsession
The agent exposed~Kurtz is gravely ill and rules through intimidation. Marlow encounters evidence of atrocity and hears Kurtz's final judgement on horror.~Marlow,Kurtz~mask~POWER reveals darkness
The comforting lie~Back in Europe, Marlow visits the Intended and conceals Kurtz's final words. His narration leaves readers confronting both imperial violence and its representation.~Marlow,The Intended~letter~EUROPE keeps its illusion''','What does Marlow conceal from the Intended?','Kurtz\'s final words; he tells her instead that Kurtz spoke her name.',note='An early modern or fin-de-siècle bridge. The critique of empire coexists with dehumanising portrayals of Africans that deserve scrutiny.')
add('The Waste Land','T. S. Eliot',1922,'1922','modern','Modernist poetry','Fragmented modern cities and mythic landscapes','Broken voices search for water and renewal.','Marie=Remembers an earlier world|The woman in the room=An anxious voice in an ornate interior|Lil=Woman discussed in the pub conversation|The typist=Figure in an empty encounter|The clerk=Partner in that encounter|Phlebas=Drowned merchant|Tiresias=Mythic witness connecting experiences', '''
The Burial of the Dead~April's renewal awakens painful memory and desire. Disconnected recollections and an unreal city introduce a spiritually exhausted world.~Marie~garden~APRIL awakens pain
A Game of Chess~An ornate interior and a pub conversation reveal strained communication, anxiety and troubled intimate lives.~The woman in the room,Lil~mirror~CONNECTION breaks
The Fire Sermon~Polluted river imagery and a mechanical encounter between typist and clerk suggest desire without meaningful connection. Tiresias links different lives and times.~The typist,The clerk,Tiresias~river~DESIRE without communion
Death by Water~Phlebas disappears beneath the sea. His brief episode strips away commercial ambition and worldly distinctions in death.~Phlebas~sea~WATER can destroy
What the Thunder Said~A parched landscape seeks rain. The thunder's commands point toward giving, compassion and self-control, but the fragmented ending offers no simple cure.~Tiresias~storm~RENEWAL remains a question''','Why can April be cruel in this poem?','Renewal awakens memories and desires that expose the pain of a spiritually exhausted world.',note='A five-part collage, not a continuous character plot. Includes the separate April note in Literature part 3; figures pictured together are thematic, not necessarily meeting.')
add('A Passage to India','E. M. Forster',1924,'1924','modern','Novel of colonial society','Chandrapore and Mau in British India','Friendship encounters the barriers of colonial rule.','Dr. Aziz=Indian physician|Cyril Fielding=English educator|Adela Quested=Visitor seeking the real India|Mrs. Moore=Visitor open to connection|Ronny Heaslop=Colonial official', '''
A meeting in the mosque~Mrs. Moore meets Aziz and treats him with respect. Their encounter suggests connection beyond official colonial attitudes.~Dr. Aziz,Mrs. Moore~temple~RESPECT opens friendship
The invitation~Fielding's gathering connects Aziz with Adela and Mrs. Moore. An excursion to the Marabar Caves follows their wish to encounter India.~Dr. Aziz,Cyril Fielding,Adela Quested~road~CONNECTION tested
The caves unsettle~Mrs. Moore is overwhelmed by the echo. Adela experiences confusion and accuses Aziz of assault; what precisely happens remains unresolved.~Adela Quested,Mrs. Moore,Dr. Aziz~cave~ECHO dissolves certainty
The accusation withdrawn~Colonial society closes ranks, but Fielding supports Aziz. At trial Adela withdraws her accusation, securing Aziz's release.~Adela Quested,Dr. Aziz,Cyril Fielding~hall~TRUTH resists the group
Not yet~Later Aziz and Fielding reconnect, yet political inequality obstructs their friendship. The landscape's refusal closes the novel with deferred possibility.~Dr. Aziz,Cyril Fielding~river~FRIENDSHIP needs equality''','What prevents an easy final friendship?','The political and racial inequality of colonial rule, beyond the goodwill of individuals.')
add('The Old Man and the Sea','Ernest Hemingway',1952,'1952','postwar','Novella','Cuba and the Gulf Stream','The catch can be lost while dignity survives.','Santiago=Ageing fisherman|Manolin=Boy devoted to Santiago|The marlin=Great fish and worthy opponent', '''
Eighty-four empty days~Santiago has caught no fish for eighty-four days. Manolin's parents move him to another boat, but the boy remains loyal to the old man.~Santiago,Manolin~home~FAILURE does not erase loyalty
Far beyond the shore~Santiago rows far out and hooks an enormous marlin. The fish pulls the skiff while the old man endures alone.~Santiago,The marlin~sea~A WORTHY opponent
Respect within struggle~For days Santiago battles pain, fatigue and the powerful fish. He admires the marlin even while preparing to kill it.~Santiago,The marlin~rope~STRUGGLE with respect
The sharks arrive~Santiago kills and lashes the marlin to the boat. Sharks attack; he fights with diminishing weapons but cannot preserve the catch.~Santiago,The marlin~shark~VICTORY stripped away
The skeleton returns~He reaches shore with only the skeleton, then sleeps and dreams of lions. Manolin cares for him and wants to fish together again.~Santiago,Manolin~home~DIGNITY survives loss''','What separates defeat from destruction here?','Santiago loses the marlin\'s flesh but retains endurance, dignity and the boy\'s loyalty.')
add('Seize the Day','Saul Bellow',1956,'1956','postwar','Psychological novella','New York during a single day','A failed investment ends in an unexpected release of grief.','Tommy Wilhelm=Man in financial and emotional crisis|Dr. Adler=His distant father|Dr. Tamkin=Dubious adviser|Margaret=Wilhelm\'s estranged wife', '''
A morning in crisis~Wilhelm is unemployed, separated from Margaret and anxious about money. Living near his father does not give him the emotional support he needs.~Tommy Wilhelm,Dr. Adler~city~CRISIS gathers
The father's refusal~Dr. Adler values discipline and success. Wilhelm's appeal for understanding instead reveals their painful inability to communicate.~Tommy Wilhelm,Dr. Adler~home~APPROVAL withheld
Tamkin offers a way~Tamkin mixes psychological advice with questionable promises. Wilhelm entrusts him with an investment in commodities, hoping for rescue.~Tommy Wilhelm,Dr. Tamkin~coins~HOPE entrusted
The money disappears~The market moves against them and Tamkin vanishes. Wilhelm cannot obtain the practical or emotional help he seeks from family.~Tommy Wilhelm,Dr. Tamkin,Margaret~storm~FINANCIAL rescue fails
An unknown man's funeral~Swept into a funeral, Wilhelm cries intensely over a stranger. Shared mortality releases grief in a moment that does not magically solve his problems.~Tommy Wilhelm~grave~GRIEF finds release''','Why is the funeral important?','It lets Wilhelm release accumulated grief through an encounter with shared mortality, without providing a neat financial rescue.')
add('Things Fall Apart','Chinua Achebe',1958,'1958','postwar','Postcolonial historical novel','Umuofia in late nineteenth-century Igboland','Personal rigidity meets a society transformed by empire.','Okonkwo=Respected man afraid of weakness|Nwoye=His son seeking another way|Ikemefuna=Boy entrusted to the household|Ezinma=His beloved daughter|Obierika=Reflective friend', '''
A reputation built on strength~Okonkwo gains status through wrestling and farming. Fear of resembling his unsuccessful father makes him harsh toward his family.~Okonkwo,Nwoye,Ezinma~village~FEAR shapes strength
Ikemefuna is killed~Despite a warning not to participate, Okonkwo helps kill the boy who calls him father. The act deepens Nwoye's estrangement.~Okonkwo,Ikemefuna,Nwoye~sword~FEAR destroys affection
Exile interrupts his rise~An accidental killing at a funeral leads to seven years of exile. During his absence, missionaries and colonial institutions gain influence.~Okonkwo,Obierika~road~EXILE changes the world
A divided return~Nwoye joins Christianity. Okonkwo returns to a community whose responses to the newcomers differ; the colonial court humiliates its leaders.~Okonkwo,Nwoye,Obierika~village~COMMUNITY fractures
Resistance without followers~Okonkwo kills a colonial messenger, but the community does not rise with him. He dies by suicide; the District Commissioner reduces his life to colonial material.~Okonkwo,Obierika~book~WHO controls the story?''','Why is the District Commissioner\'s ending significant?','He reduces a complex life and culture to material for a colonial account, showing power over representation as well as institutions.')

